    {{!-- GOLF --}}

    <div class="game_name game1">
        <div class="game_imgs">
            <img class="img1" src="images/golf_1.png" alt="">
            <h4>Wanna Play!</h4>

            <img class="img2" src="images/golf_2.png" alt="">

        </div>

        <div class="game_title are1">
            <h3> GOLF</h3>
            <p>
                Golf is a popular precision sport played individually or in teams, where the objective is to complete a
                course in as few strokes as possible. Here's a brief overview of golf:

                <br> <b> Course:</b> Golf is played on a vast outdoor course typically consisting of 18 holes, though
                shorter 9-hole
                courses are also common. Each hole has a teeing area, fairway, rough, hazards (such as bunkers and water
                hazards), and a green with a flagstick and a hole.

                <br> <b> Equipment:</b> Golfers use a variety of clubs, each with different designs and purposes, to hit
                the golf ball.
                The primary clubs include drivers, irons, wedges, and putters. Golf balls are specially designed for
                distance, control, and feel.

                <br> <b> Scoring: </b>The objective is to complete the course in as few strokes as possible. The player
                or team with the
                lowest total number of strokes at the end of the round wins. Scoring is based on the number of strokes
                taken
                to get the ball from the teeing area into the hole.

                <br> <b> Teeing Off:</b> Each hole starts with a "tee shot" from the teeing area. Golfers aim to hit the
                ball as far and
                accurately as possible to position it for subsequent shots.

                <br><b> Fairway and Green:</b> After the tee shot, players try to advance the ball closer to the hole
                with each stroke.
                The fairway is the well-maintained area leading to the green, where the grass is shorter. The green is a
                finely manicured area around the hole, and putting strokes are used to complete the hole.

                <br> <b>Hazards:</b> Golf courses often have bunkers (sand traps) and water hazards, which add to the
                challenge. Players
                must strategize to avoid these obstacles.

                <br> <b> Etiquette:</b> Golf is known for its emphasis on etiquette and sportsmanship. Players are
                expected to maintain a
                high level of respect for fellow golfers and the course. It's customary to be quiet and avoid
                distractions
                when others are playing.

                <br> <b> Handicap System:</b> To enable players of different skill levels to compete fairly, a handicap
                system is used in
                golf. This system provides a measure of a player's ability relative to a scratch golfer and adjusts the
                number of strokes they receive or give in various formats.

                <br> <b> Professional Tours:</b> Golf has prominent professional tours, including the PGA Tour
                (Professional Golfers'
                Association) in the United States and international events like The Open Championship, the Masters
                Tournament, and the Ryder Cup, which feature the world's top golfers.

                <br> <b> Recreational and Social:</b> Golf is enjoyed by people of all ages and skill levels, and it is
                often played for
                both leisure and business purposes. It provides an opportunity for physical activity, social
                interaction,
                and relaxation in a natural setting.

                Golf is a sport that demands precision, skill, and strategy, and it has a dedicated following worldwide.
                It
                can be played at various levels, from casual weekend rounds to highly competitive professional
                tournaments.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    {{!-- tennis --}}

    <div class="game_name">
        <div class="game_title">
            <h3>tennis</h3>
            <p>
                <b> Tennis </b>is a racquet sport played on a rectangular court divided by a net. Here are some key
                points:

                <br><b> Objective:</b> Players aim to score points by hitting a ball over the net and into their
                opponent's court. The
                goal is to win games, sets, and matches.

                <br> <b>Court:</b> Tennis can be played on various surfaces, including grass, clay, and hard courts. The
                court is
                divided into two halves, and players take turns serving and returning from opposite sides.

                <br> <b> Equipment:</b> Players use a tennis racquet to hit a rubber ball. The choice of racquet and
                ball can vary based
                on player preference and court surface.

                <br> <b>Scoring:</b> Tennis uses a unique scoring system with points like "love," "15," "30," and "40."
                To win a game, a
                player or doubles team must score four points and lead by at least two points.

                <br> <b> Matches:</b> Matches can consist of best-of-three sets or best-of-five sets, depending on the
                level of play. To
                win a set, a player or team must win at least six games with a two-game lead.

                <br> <b> MajorTournaments:</b> Tennis features prestigious events known as Grand Slam tournaments,
                including the
                Australian Open, the French Open, Wimbledon, and the US Open. These events attract top players and
                significant global attention.

                <br> <b> Singles and Doubles:</b> Tennis can be played as singles (one player per side) or doubles (two
                players per
                side). Doubles teams collaborate to serve, return, and cover the court effectively.

                <br> <b> Professional Tours:</b> The ATP Tour (for men) and the WTA Tour (for women) are the primary
                professional
                circuits, where players compete for rankings and titles.

                <br> <b> Technique and Strategy:</b> Tennis requires a combination of power, agility, and finesse.
                Players employ
                different strategies, such as baseline play, serve-and-volley, and defensive tactics.

                <br><b> Global Popularity:</b> Tennis is a widely followed and played sport globally, with a strong
                international fan
                base. It is also an Olympic sport.

                Tennis is known for its fast-paced rallies, intense competition, and the diverse range of playing styles
                it
                accommodates, making it a popular sport at both recreational and professional levels.
                Tennis is a popular racquet sport played on a rectangular court, typically in singles (one player per
                side) or doubles (two players per side). The objective is to score points by hitting a ball over a net
                and into the opponent's court while following specific rules. Tennis is known for its fast-paced,
                competitive nature and is played at both amateur and professional levels, with major tournaments like
                Wimbledon, the US Open, the French Open, and the Australian Open attracting top players from around the
                world.
            </p>
        </div>

